WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.010
A High baseline effort depends on a precise system boundary.

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:08.702
The boundary identifies the cloud service offering in scope, including application components,

00:00:08.702 --> 00:00:12.221
infrastructure, data stores, identity services, administrative interfaces, network paths,

00:00:12.221 --> 00:00:14.958
monitoring tools, operational processes, and supporting services.

00:00:14.958 --> 00:00:20.041
If the boundary is unclear, the team cannot reliably explain which controls apply,

00:00:20.041 --> 00:00:23.560
which evidence is relevant, or who owns each responsibility.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.297
Dependencies deserve special attention in high-impact environments.

00:00:26.297 --> 00:00:29.816
External services, third-party tools, inherited platform capabilities, support systems,

00:00:29.816 --> 00:00:34.117
build pipelines, logging services, and backup locations can all affect risk.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:38.027
A dependency may provide a control the service relies on,

00:00:38.027 --> 00:00:41.546
or it may introduce a weakness that needs mitigation.

00:00:41.546 --> 00:00:46.238
Either way, it must be visible and supportable in the evidence story.

00:00:46.238 --> 00:00:48.584
Privileged administration paths are especially important.

00:00:48.584 --> 00:00:54.058
Teams need to know who can administer the service, how administrative access is authenticated,

00:00:54.058 --> 00:00:57.968
how privileges are approved and reviewed, how actions are logged,

00:00:57.968 --> 00:01:00.314
and how emergency access is handled.

00:01:00.314 --> 00:01:02.660
The same applies to customer-managed responsibilities.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.352
If customers must configure users, data handling, network access, or integrations securely,

00:01:07.352 --> 00:01:10.089
those responsibilities should be plain and traceable.

00:01:10.089 --> 00:01:13.217
Unclear dependencies create evidence gaps and risk misunderstandings.

00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:17.909
A provider may assume the platform handles a requirement while the platform

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:21.037
scope does not cover the provider's use case.

00:01:21.037 --> 00:01:25.729
A customer may assume a protection is automatic when it requires configuration.

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:26.511
For High,

00:01:26.511 --> 00:01:31.203
these misunderstandings can be unacceptable because the consequences of failure are serious.

00:01:31.203 --> 00:01:33.940
Boundary clarity is part of risk control.
